 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.crisisdeuda.02.de-un-dia-para-otro",
-  "title": "De un día para otro: un cambio repentino",
+  "title": "What had already happened: the pluperfect",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*De un día para otro* (overnight, from one day to the next) emphasizes how suddenly a change happened -- useful when a reading wants to stress that there was no gradual transition, just an abrupt before and after. It's an adverbial phrase that can appear at various points in a sentence. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know the *pluscuamperfecto* from B1 Unit 1: *había* + a past participle, for something that had already happened before another moment in the past. In the story of the 1982 crisis it shows what had happened before the crisis broke out."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "El costo de esa deuda se disparó, de un día para otro, sin que los países hubieran hecho nada distinto.",
-          "english": "The cost of that debt shot up, overnight, without the countries having done anything different."
+          "spanish": "Antes de 1979, muchos países ya habían acumulado una gran deuda.",
