 {
   "id": "grammar.b1.crisisdeuda.04.en-detrimento-de",
-  "title": "En detrimento de: quién o qué sale perjudicado",
+  "title": "As prices rose: cuanto más and a medida que",
   "sections": [
     {
       "type": "text",
-      "content": "*En detrimento de* (to the detriment of) names who or what suffers as a result of a decision that benefits something else -- useful for describing policy trade-offs where one priority is favored at another's expense. It's followed by a noun phrase. These sentences are taken directly from the previous reading."
+      "title": "What you already know",
+      "content": "You know *a medida que* (“as”) and *cuanto más …, más …* (“the more …, the more …”) from B1 Unit 2. Both link two changes that grow together. They suit the social cost of the 1980s, when prices, unemployment and poverty changed at the same time."
     },
     {
       "type": "examples",
       "items": [
         {
-          "spanish": "Los recortes al gasto público, en detrimento sobre todo de la salud y la educación, se combinaron con una inflación descontrolada.",
-          "english": "Cuts to public spending, to the detriment especially of health and education, combined with uncontrolled inflation."
+          "spanish": "A medida que los precios subían, los salarios compraban menos.",
